om2
note tools/forge.om: the Omega forge's page generator, in pure seeded Omega.
note
note Radicle already holds the repositories, issues and patches, signed by their authors and copied
note peer to peer. This program turns what one seed node knows about them, and the pulls the seed
note served, into a static web site: one index of every project ranked by pulls, one page per
note project, one per issue and one per patch, and stats.tsv for programs. The pages have no
note script, no cookie and no outside request; the forms post to the seed's own intake
note (tools/intake.om). Build with the seed-born compiler:
note   omc < tools/forge.om > forge && chmod +x forge
note   { printf 'target x86linux\n'; cat tools/forge.om; } | omc-x86 > forge   (native x86-64)
note Run:
note   forge FACTS LEDGER DAY OUT
note   FACTS   read: what the seed node knows, written by tools/forge-cycle.sh. The first line is
note           exactly "forge-facts 1"; then one record per line, TAB-separated, in any order:
note             seed TAB URL           exactly once: the base of the seed's git over HTTPS, so
note                                    that URL ZID ".git" clones a project; 1..200 bytes of
note                                    A-Z a-z 0-9 : / . _ - ~
note             project TAB RID TAB HEAD TAB JSON
note                                    a project: its RID, its default-branch head (40 lowercase
note                                    hex) and the JSON of `rad inspect --payload RID` on one line
note             seeder TAB RID TAB NID one node that seeds the project (`rad node routing`)
note             issue TAB RID TAB ID TAB JSON
note             patch TAB RID TAB ID TAB JSON
note                                    an issue or patch of a project: its id (40 lowercase hex)
note                                    and the JSON of `rad cob show` for it, on one line
note           RID is "rad:" then a ZID; a ZID or a NID is "z" then 20..60 base58 digits. A project
note           appears once, a (RID, NID) or a (RID, ID) pair once; every RID a seeder, issue or
note           patch names is a project's. Every JSON value is an object, structurally checked
note           (strings, escapes, bracket nesting at most 64 deep). At most 16777136 bytes, 1024
note           projects, 16384 issues and patches, 16384 seeders. Every line ends in a newline: an
note           empty file, a blank line, a CR anywhere or a last line without its newline is
note           refused.
note   LEDGER  read: the pull ledger of tools/tally.om. Header exactly
note             rid TAB day TAB https TAB hosts TAB rad TAB peers
note           then rows RID TAB DAY TAB four decimal counts, strictly ascending by (RID, DAY), with
note           hosts <= https and peers <= rad. Rows for a RID that is not a project are skipped.
note           Read in 64 KiB pieces, so it may be of any size; a line is at most 65535 bytes.
note   DAY     today, YYYY-MM-DD (UTC). "14 days" means DAY and the 13 days before it.
note   OUT     an existing empty directory. Written: index.html, stats.tsv and for every project
note           ZID/index.html, ZID/issue-ID.html and ZID/patch-ID.html. Files and directories are
note           created new (O_EXCL), never overwritten.
note Every text byte that comes from FACTS goes through one escape (the model proved in
note tests/forge/Forge.lean): & < > " ' become &amp; &lt; &gt; &quot; &#39;, a control byte
note other than LF and TAB becomes &#xFFFD;, every other byte is copied. JSON strings are decoded
note first (\uXXXX and surrogate pairs to UTF-8, a lone surrogate to U+FFFD).
note Ranking on the index: pulls in 14 days, then pulls of all time, both descending, then RID.
note On a project page issues and patches are listed open first, newest first, then by id.
note A patch revision's gate verdict is the last comment on it that starts "flow gate pass " or
note "flow gate fail " (posted by tools/flow.om's queue).
note Exit 0 when every page is written; 2 for the wrong number of arguments, with the usage line on
note stderr; 1 for a refusal, with one stderr line "forge: ..." naming the argument or the file
note and its line. FACTS and LEDGER are checked before the first page is written; a later failure
note (a page over 8 MiB, a write error) can leave OUT partly written, so tools/forge-cycle.sh
note always generates into a fresh directory and publishes it only after exit 0. The output depends
note only on the arguments and the input bytes: no clock, no environment.

